00:00:03.369 --> 00:00:07.120 align:start position:0%
soldiers arrive in San Ignacio to help  combat wildfires Bolivia's famous  colonial jewel settled by Jesuit  missionaries in the late 1600s is been  converted into an Operations Center for  the firefighting effort night and day  the wildfires are spreading out of  control fanned by strong winds a  prolonged drought and extremely high  temperatures we feel sad to see how the  animals in the forest are burning but we  are proud to be able to help these  soldiers are going off again to fight  the fires but as you can see they're not  very well equipped they don't look like  firemen they don't have the equipment of  firemen their boots have rubber soles  that can easily melt in the flames and  in the intense heat and they also don't  have the proper masks to protect them  from vi smoke and from smoke inhalation  thousands of young volunteers are  joining them like acento Rivera from  Ecuador never fought fires before but we  will use the nerves of steel and  adrenaline to do it this fire isn't just  the Bolivian problem it's all of ours  because the tropical forest is  fundamental for our survival  this part of Eastern Bolivia is cattle  country nearly two months ago the  government signed a decree authorizing  ranchers to expand the grazing frontier  using fire to clear the tropical forests  cattle is good business says the mayor  the President signed the decree in the  presence of the leaders of the cattle  ranchers they don't take into account  the weather or the fact that a drought  makes it very dangerous to clear the  land with fire they don't care and this  is the result  the decree hasn't been rescinded despite  more than 1.5 million hectares Bolivia's  Amazon and Chiquita no tropical forests  destroyed so far by fire volunteers pray  for God to send them rain and for a time  it seems their prayers are answered the  mare is jubilant the light shower is  insufficient to stop the catastrophe but  given the circumstances people here  believe every drop counts  Lucia Neumann San Ignacio Bolivia
 

